{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786434417990,"sessionID":"ses_010495952ffeknG4pENad6KM6w","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_tdBKkXeDYggMR04kFDhaDEbS","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"17-section|## 17\\.|## 16\\.|## 15\\.|## 14\\.|## 13\\.","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research"},"output":"Found 10 matches\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/assets/deep-research-strategy.md:\n  Line 173: ## 13. RESEARCH BOUNDARIES\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/references/guides/quick-reference.md:\n  Line 236: ## 13. REVIEW MODE\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/feature-catalog/loop-lifecycle/synthesis.md:\n  Line 27: The synthesis phase reads every iteration file plus the final strategy state, then writes the canonical `research/research.md` document in the standard 17-section format. This phase owns deduplication and final organization even when progressive synthesis has already been updating the file during earlier iterations.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md:\n  Line 535: 3. **Compile research/research.md**: Merge findings into 17-section format\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md:\n  Line 86: Synthesis owns the final `research/research.md` output. It reads all iteration files and the final strategy state, compiles the standard 17-section research document, adds the required `Eliminated Alternatives` table, marks the config status complete, and appends a `synthesis_complete` event to the JSONL log.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/feature-catalog/research-output/progressive-synthesis.md:\n  Line 29: The final workflow still owns synthesis. After convergence, the synth phase reads all iteration files and the final strategy state, deduplicates overlap, and cleans the document into the canonical 17-section structure. If `progressiveSynthesis` is disabled, the loop skips iteration-time writes and creates `research/research.md` from scratch during synthesis, but the file remains the workflow-owned final research surface in both modes.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:\n  Line 708: ## 13. COMMAND FLOW STRESS TESTS\n\n  Line 762: ## 14. FAN-OUT\n\n  Line 807: ## 15. AUTOMATED TEST CROSS-REFERENCE\n\n  Line 814: ## 15. FEATURE CATALOG CROSS-REFERENCE INDEX\n","metadata":{"matches":10,"truncated":false},"title":"17-section|## 17\\.|## 16\\.|## 15\\.|## 14\\.|## 13\\.","time":{"start":1786434417955,"end":1786434417988}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0bfe78f8c056ed47016a7ad3717bc08191bcbaa2d55371425b"}},"id":"prt_fefc9f498001kF8l87zhjfkYfW","sessionID":"ses_010495952ffeknG4pENad6KM6w","messageID":"msg_fefc9bf910017arldzBuaxZR75"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786434418178,"sessionID":"ses_010495952ffeknG4pENad6KM6w","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_G79nShwpyhjrC0L3hiLqilyw","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/specs/cli-external-orchestration/042-improved-communication/001-research-strategy/research/lineages/gpt-sol-fast/deep-research-strategy.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/specs/cli-external-orchestration/042-improved-communication/001-research-strategy/research/lineages/gpt-sol-fast/deep-research-strategy.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Deep Research Strategy: Provider-Neutral CLI Display Projection\n2: \n3: ## 2. TOPIC\n4: Reverse engineer claudish-to-english and derive an evidence-backed, provider-neutral display-projection architecture that preserves canonical runtime state while matching its direct 1:1 communication feel across Claude CLI, Codex CLI, Pi CLI, OpenCode CLI, Devin CLI, and Cursor CLI.\n5: \n6: <!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n7: ## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\n8: - [x] What is the safest primary-source-supported display integration boundary in each of the six CLIs, and how should their events normalize without mutating canonical context, tools, or transcripts?\n9: - [x] How should message assembly handle streaming, ordering, duplication, concurrency, cancellation, timeout, retry, and atomic render commitment?\n10: - [x] Which protected-span and semantic fidelity gates can reject every changed literal, structure, fact, polarity, requirement strength, caveat, or next step and return the exact original?\n11: - [x] How should privacy-aware routing normalize OpenCode Go DeepSeek V4 Flash, Ollama, llama.cpp, and other hosted or local providers without unapproved egress or capability assumptions?\n12: - [x] Which observability, perceptual-parity evaluation, and downstream phase boundaries make the architecture testable and implementable?\n13: \n14: <!-- /ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n15: \n16: ## 4. NON-GOALS\n17: - No production implementation, provider configuration, package installation, service startup, or deployment.\n18: - No mutation of canonical model context, tool events, tool results, transcript state, or the read-only reference.\n19: - No claim that protocol compatibility alone proves privacy, fidelity, or safe presentation replacement.\n20: - No writes outside this detached lineage packet.\n21: \n22: ## 5. STOP CONDITIONS\n23: - Complete exactly three evidence iterations, regardless of early convergence telemetry.\n24: - Stop on an unrecoverable state or scope violation and preserve the partial packet honestly.\n25: \n26: <!-- ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n27: ## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n28: - What is the safest primary-source-supported display integration boundary in each of the six CLIs, and how should their events normalize without mutating canonical context, tools, or transcripts?\n29: - How should message assembly handle streaming, ordering, duplication, concurrency, cancellation, timeout, retry, and atomic render commitment?\n30: - Which protected-span and semantic fidelity gates can reject every changed literal, structure, fact, polarity, requirement strength, caveat, or next step and return the exact original?\n31: - How should privacy-aware routing normalize OpenCode Go DeepSeek V4 Flash, Ollama, llama.cpp, and other hosted or local providers without unapproved egress or capability assumptions?\n32: - Which observability, perceptual-parity evaluation, and downstream phase boundaries make the architecture testable and implementable?\n33: \n34: <!-- /ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n35: \n36: <!-- MACHINE-OWNED: START -->\n37: <!-- ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n38: ## 7. WHAT WORKED\n39: - runtime-owned client protocols exposed identity, lifecycle, cancellation, and terminal states more completely than hooks, while Claude’s dedicated display hook provided the useful narrow exception. (iteration 1)\n40: - transport standards separated source ordering, correlation, reconnection, and terminal behavior; CommonMark exposed where parsed structure loses source bytes; OWASP supported an asymmetric deterministic-first trust boundary. (iteration 2)\n41: - provider-native documents exposed where compatibility ends, while dated privacy terms made policy freshness an explicit routing input rather than prose. (iteration 3)\n42: \n43: <!-- /ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n44: \n45: <!-- ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n46: ## 8. WHAT FAILED\n47: - broad rendered pages produced oversized captures, and one assumed ACP documentation route did not exist. Raw primary documents and runtime-specific pages were more reliable. (iteration 1)\n48: - no standard can make probabilistic semantic comparison a proof, and CommonMark cannot define runtime-specific table syntax. Both gaps must become fail-closed capability/fixture requirements rather than optimistic assumptions. (iteration 2)\n49: - the former OpenTelemetry page had moved, and mutable provider/server pages cannot prove installed behavior. The maintained repository supplied the telemetry abstraction; mutable capabilities became pinned probes. (iteration 3)\n50: \n51: <!-- /ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n52: \n53: <!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n54: ## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n55: ### `https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/session-updates` returned 404. No claims depend on that unavailable page; shared ACP semantics beyond the runtime-specific documentation remain unconfirmed. -- BLOCKED (iteration 1, 1 attempts)\n56: - What was tried: `https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/session-updates` returned 404. No claims depend on that unavailable page; shared ACP semantics beyond the runtime-specific documentation remain unconfirmed.\n57: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n58: - Do NOT retry: `https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/session-updates` returned 404. No claims depend on that unavailable page; shared ACP semantics beyond the runtime-specific documentation remain unconfirmed.\n59: \n60: ### A deterministic gate cannot prove every semantic equivalence claim. The safe resolution is asymmetric: deterministic checks can approve literal/structural invariants, while semantic uncertainty can only reject and fall back. [SOURCE: https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/] [INFERENCE: fail-closed consequence of non-fool-proof model behavior] -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n61: - What was tried: A deterministic gate cannot prove every semantic equivalence claim. The safe resolution is asymmetric: deterministic checks can approve literal/structural invariants, while semantic uncertainty can only reject and fall back. [SOURCE: https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/] [INFERENCE: fail-closed consequence of non-fool-proof model behavior]\n62: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n63: - Do NOT retry: A deterministic gate cannot prove every semantic equivalence claim. The safe resolution is asymmetric: deterministic checks can approve literal/structural invariants, while semantic uncertainty can only reject and fall back. [SOURCE: https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/] [INFERENCE: fail-closed consequence of non-fool-proof model behavior]\n64: \n65: ### A localhost-shaped URL cannot prove offline execution: Ollama compatibility documents a localhost example, while llama.cpp separately exposes host, offline, model-download, and remote-server controls. Deployment and egress must be recorded and enforced independently. [SOURCE: https://docs.ollama.com/api/openai-compatibility] [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/master/tools/server/README.md] -- BLOCKED (iteration 3, 1 attempts)\n66: - What was tried: A localhost-shaped URL cannot prove offline execution: Ollama compatibility documents a localhost example, while llama.cpp separately exposes host, offline, model-download, and remote-server controls. Deployment and egress must be recorded and enforced independently. [SOURCE: https://docs.ollama.com/api/openai-compatibility] [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/master/tools/server/README.md]\n67: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n68: - Do NOT retry: A localhost-shaped URL cannot prove offline execution: Ollama compatibility documents a localhost example, while llama.cpp separately exposes host, offline, model-download, and remote-server controls. Deployment and egress must be recorded and enforced independently. [SOURCE: https://docs.ollama.com/api/openai-compatibility] [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/master/tools/server/README.md]\n69: \n70: ### A narrow grep over the captured Claude web response exceeded the tool record limit. The official page’s directly returned lifecycle table and the local reference supplied the used evidence. -- BLOCKED (iteration 1, 1 attempts)\n71: - What was tried: A narrow grep over the captured Claude web response exceeded the tool record limit. The official page’s directly returned lifecycle table and the local reference supplied the used evidence.\n72: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n73: - Do NOT retry: A narrow grep over the captured Claude web response exceeded the tool record limit. The official page’s directly returned lifecycle table and the local reference supplied the used evidence.\n74: \n75: ### A single universal lifecycle hook: the six runtimes expose materially different boundaries; only Claude confirms a dedicated presentation-only hook. [SOURCE: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks] [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/server/] -- BLOCKED (iteration 1, 1 attempts)\n76: - What was tried: A single universal lifecycle hook: the six runtimes expose materially different boundaries; only Claude confirms a dedicated presentation-only hook. [SOURCE: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks] [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/server/]\n77: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n78: - Do NOT retry: A single universal lifecycle hook: the six runtimes expose materially different boundaries; only Claude confirms a dedicated presentation-only hook. [SOURCE: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks] [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/server/]\n79: \n80: ### A universal table parser is not available from CommonMark because tables are a dialect extension. Version-pinned runtime fixtures must declare Markdown dialect and parser version before implementation. [SOURCE: https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/] -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n81: - What was tried: A universal table parser is not available from CommonMark because tables are a dialect extension. Version-pinned runtime fixtures must declare Markdown dialect and parser version before implementation. [SOURCE: https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/]\n82: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n83: - Do NOT retry: A universal table parser is not available from CommonMark because tables are a dialect extension. Version-pinned runtime fixtures must declare Markdown dialect and parser version before implementation. [SOURCE: https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/]\n84: \n85: ### Arrival order as canonical assembly order: JSON-RPC permits concurrent batch responses in any order, and SSE reconnects can replay from `Last-Event-ID`. [SOURCE: https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification] [SOURCE: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html] -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n86: - What was tried: Arrival order as canonical assembly order: JSON-RPC permits concurrent batch responses in any order, and SSE reconnects can replay from `Last-Event-ID`. [SOURCE: https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification] [SOURCE: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html]\n87: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n88: - Do NOT retry: Arrival order as canonical assembly order: JSON-RPC permits concurrent batch responses in any order, and SSE reconnects can replay from `Last-Event-ID`. [SOURCE: https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification] [SOURCE: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html]\n89: \n90: ### Automatic local-to-hosted fallback, even to a zero-retention route, because egress consent and residency remain separate decisions. [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/go/] [INFERENCE: privacy-class and consent boundary] -- BLOCKED (iteration 3, 1 attempts)\n91: - What was tried: Automatic local-to-hosted fallback, even to a zero-retention route, because egress consent and residency remain separate decisions. [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/go/] [INFERENCE: privacy-class and consent boundary]\n92: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n93: - Do NOT retry: Automatic local-to-hosted fallback, even to a zero-retention route, because egress consent and residency remain separate decisions. [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/go/] [INFERENCE: privacy-class and consent boundary]\n94: \n95: ### Automatic readability or model-judge scores as proof of semantic or perceptual parity. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/042-improved-communication/001-research-strategy/research/lineages/gpt-sol-fast/iterations/iteration-002.md:26] [INFERENCE: diagnostic metrics cannot authorize a projection] -- BLOCKED (iteration 3, 1 attempts)\n96: - What was tried: Automatic readability or model-judge scores as proof of semantic or perceptual parity. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/042-improved-communication/001-research-strategy/research/lineages/gpt-sol-fast/iterations/iteration-002.md:26] [INFERENCE: diagnostic metrics cannot authorize a projection]\n97: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n98: - Do NOT retry: Automatic readability or model-judge scores as proof of semantic or perceptual parity. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/042-improved-communication/001-research-strategy/research/lineages/gpt-sol-fast/iterations/iteration-002.md:26] [INFERENCE: diagnostic metrics cannot authorize a projection]\n99: \n100: ### Content-bearing telemetry or raw provider errors; lifecycle enums and keyed correlation are sufficient for operations. [SOURCE: https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai] [INFERENCE: data-minimization consequence] -- BLOCKED (iteration 3, 1 attempts)\n101: - What was tried: Content-bearing telemetry or raw provider errors; lifecycle enums and keyed correlation are sufficient for operations. [SOURCE: https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai] [INFERENCE: data-minimization consequence]\n102: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n103: - Do NOT retry: Content-bearing telemetry or raw provider errors; lifecycle enums and keyed correlation are sufficient for operations. [SOURCE: https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai] [INFERENCE: data-minimization consequence]\n104: \n105: ### Current mutable documentation cannot substitute for version-pinned provider/build/model fixtures. [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/go/] [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/master/tools/server/README.md] -- BLOCKED (iteration 3, 1 attempts)\n106: - What was tried: Current mutable documentation cannot substitute for version-pinned provider/build/model fixtures. [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/go/] [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/master/tools/server/README.md]\n107: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n108: - Do NOT retry: Current mutable documentation cannot substitute for version-pinned provider/build/model fixtures. [SOURCE: https://opencode.ai/docs/go/] [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/master/tools/server/README.md]\n109: \n110: ### Pi finalized-message replacement as the projector: the documented handler can replace the lifecycle message, violating the immutable-canonical-lane requirement. [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md] -- BLOCKED (iteration 1, 1 attempts)\n111: - What was tried: Pi finalized-message replacement as the projector: the documented handler can replace the lifecycle message, violating the immutable-canonical-lane requirement. [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md]\n112: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n113: - Do NOT retry: Pi finalized-message replacement as the projector: the documented handler can replace the lifecycle message, violating the immutable-canonical-lane requirement. [SOURCE: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md]\n114: \n115: ### Reconstructing the original from placeholders, a Markdown AST, or decoded text: parsing and decoding can erase byte-level distinctions. [SOURCE: https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/] [SOURCE: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html] -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n116: - What was tried: Reconstructing the original from placeholders, a Markdown AST, or decoded text: parsing and decoding can erase byte-level distinctions. [SOURCE: https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/] [SOURCE: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html]\n117: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n118: - Do NOT retry: Reconstructing the original from placeholders, a Markdown AST, or decoded text: parsing and decoding can erase byte-level distinctions. [SOURCE: https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/] [SOURCE: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html]\n119: \n120: ### Reusing Cursor’s ACP field mapping unchanged for Devin: both expose ACP, but the consulted Devin source confirms transport rather than identical update payloads. [SOURCE: https://docs.devin.ai/cli/reference/commands] [SOURCE: https://cursor.com/docs/cli/acp] -- BLOCKED (iteration 1, 1 attempts)\n121: - What was tried: Reusing Cursor’s ACP field mapping unchanged for Devin: both expose ACP, but the consulted Devin source confirms transport rather than identical update payloads. [SOURCE: https://docs.devin.ai/cli/reference/commands] [SOURCE: https://cursor.com/docs/cli/acp]\n122: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n123: - Do NOT retry: Reusing Cursor’s ACP field mapping unchanged for Devin: both expose ACP, but the consulted Devin source confirms transport rather than identical update payloads. [SOURCE: https://docs.devin.ai/cli/reference/commands] [SOURCE: https://cursor.com/docs/cli/acp]\n124: \n125: ### Suppressing original chunks until a rewrite succeeds: it turns missing-final, timeout, cancellation, and validator failure into blank or reconstructed output rather than exact fallback. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/042-improved-communication/001-research-strategy/research/lineages/gpt-sol-fast/iterations/iteration-001.md:30] -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n126: - What was tried: Suppressing original chunks until a rewrite succeeds: it turns missing-final, timeout, cancellation, and validator failure into blank or reconstructed output rather than exact fallback. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/042-improved-communication/001-research-strategy/research/lineages/gpt-sol-fast/iterations/iteration-001.md:30]\n127: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n128: - Do NOT retry: Suppressing original chunks until a rewrite succeeds: it turns missing-final, timeout, cancellation, and validator failure into blank or reconstructed output rather than e